TEG: The Trans-Existential Grounding framework; Geir Isene <https://isene.com/freewill/>
	The framework in one line
		Existence requires a ground outside itself, and that ground must choose
	The two halves are not held with equal confidence, by design
		The requirement is PROVED <Paper 1: Why Existence Cannot Ground Itself>
		The identification is ARGUED, by elimination <Paper 2: The Ground Must Choose>
	Paper 1: Why Existence Cannot Ground Itself (Gödelian incompleteness and the structure of physical law)
		CLAIM: The lawful structure of existence cannot be self-supporting
		Definition: Existence (E) = everything that has properties
			Physical entities and their laws; thoughts, concepts, numbers, proofs
			Deliberately generous; closes the Platonic exit at the start
		Existence has formal structure
			Physical law is expressed mathematically, and the expression works
				Newton: arithmetic relations; Relativity: differential geometry; Quantum mechanics: Hilbert spaces
			Wigner: "the unreasonable effectiveness of mathematics" (he declined to explain it)
			Regularities survive any change of notation; not artefacts of description
		The structural argument (two premises and a theorem)
			1 The lawful structure of E constitutes a formal system containing arithmetic
			2 E is consistent (empirical; every test we have run supports it)
			THEOREM: The grounding requirement
				[? E's lawful structure is a consistent formal system containing arithmetic] E cannot establish its own consistency
				PROOF: Gödel's second incompleteness theorem, applied to S = the lawful structure of E
				Any account of E's consistency must appeal to something outside E
				The gap is structural, not epistemic; no complete set of internal laws can close it
			The disjunctive form (grant that E merely CONTAINS formal systems)
				[? E is a formal system] the theorem applies as stated
				[? E is not a formal system] a non-formal substrate reliably produces formal structure, which needs its own account
				Either way the order is not self-accounting
			The pattern behind the theorem
				Cantor: no enumeration captures all reals
				Gödel: no consistent system proves its own consistency
				Tarski: no language defines its own truth
				Lawvere: all three are one categorical fact, so ascending to richer theories never escapes
		Three further routes, and what each adds
			The Leibnizian route (sufficient reason)
				Contingent things want reasons; a reason from inside E is itself contingent
				The chain terminates only in something necessary, and nothing inside E is necessary
			The property regress
				Complex properties reduce to simpler ones, down to fundamental constants
				Ask why THOSE; three exits only
					1 Terminates in something with no properties (outside E by Definition 1)
					2 Descends forever (each link borrows what it has yet to receive)
					3 Stops at properties simply had (brute fact relocated)
				Runs on a dependency relation; needs no Gödel, no Leibniz, no principle of explanation
			The exhaustive why
				Every answer cites a prior condition; only what needs no why terminates the chain
				Sufficient reason carried out as a procedure; not counted separately
			What the routes owe each other
				Three genuinely separate starting points: Gödel, the property regress, sufficient reason
				Each fixes one constraint on the ground
					Gödel: OUTSIDE the system it grounds
					Sufficient reason: NECESSARY
					Property regress: PROPERTY-FREE
					Exhaustive why: NEEDS NO FURTHER WHY
				The constraints turn out jointly satisfiable; that is the interesting fact
		Why the regress does not terminate inside existence
			Four conditions on any candidate ground
				Transcendence; Productive capacity; No determinate properties; Sustains consistency
			The candidates, and where each fails
				A deity with attributes; fails on properties ("why loving rather than cruel?" is the fine-structure question again)
				Platonic forms; fail productive capacity (the number seven does not do anything)
				The quantum vacuum; fails transcendence (a state of quantised fields; calling it "nothing" makes the word do the work)
				The multiverse; more of the thing to be explained is not an explanation of it
				Emergence; presupposes parts, relations and laws, exactly what needs grounding
				A necessary being; maximal properties resume the regress (right about the termination, wrong to give it a character)
				Brute fact; always available, never informs; the one move that guarantees no progress
			The pattern in the failures
				Every candidate is inside E, or carries determinate character that puts it back inside E
		What survives
			Constraints, not an identification: outside E, property-free, able to actualise, able to sustain order
			Naming the ground is a further question <Paper 2: The Ground Must Choose>
	Paper 2: The Ground Must Choose (free will as trans-existential ground)
		CLAIM: Only unrestricted decision meets the four conditions
		The bridge: which of the available possibilities becomes actual?
			Something settles this; call the settling selection
			Mechanism fails on properties
				A fixed procedure is a determinate property; the ground lands back inside E
				A rule-run ground is a formal system; <The structural argument> applies to it whole
			Chance fails on order
				Structure persists: constants hold, chemistry repeats, mathematics does not fluctuate
				Random actualisation gives drift; "randomness within constraints" concedes the point (constraints are rules)
			What remains
				Selection neither forced nor arbitrary: choice in the minimal sense
				THEOREM: The selection requirement
					[? the four conditions hold] actualisation is neither deterministic nor random
					The determinism-or-luck dichotomy was never exhaustive; the third option lives outside existence
		The framework
			P = pure potential (what has no properties); W = the capacity for unrestricted decision, within P
			W and E are disjoint by construction; the real claim: something can be real with no properties at all
			Actualisation is a map from decisions and moments to actualised states of affairs
				Continuous, not a single originating act; sustaining and originating are one operation
				Not computable; a computation is rule-governed and <THEOREM: The selection requirement> excludes it
				E is the IMAGE of actualisation: no prior arena, no furniture to rearrange
				Existence emerges from free will; free will does not emerge from existence
			Remark: the empty set has no members, yet the whole cumulative hierarchy builds from it
		Indeterminacy is real
			Classical: no physical state is specified to infinite precision ("pervasive indefiniteness", Potter, Ellis & Mitchell)
			Quantum: Bell violations, loophole-free; local hidden variables excluded
			Bohm answered by finite information; Many-Worlds answered by the multiverse objection
			Superdeterminism is the brute fact move wearing local dress
		Constraint from one's own choices
			Each actualisation converts potential into something definite, and the definite must be maintained
			Volitional entropy: accumulated commitment of the actualiser, felt as constraint
			The chess analogy: the rules arrived with the decision to play (laws of physics as standing commitments)
			A long-actualising chooser SHOULD feel exactly as constrained as we do
		Computation cannot choose
			A deterministic machine is mechanism; adding a random source is chance; both are excluded
			[!] No claim against capability: behaviour is a poor guide to the presence of W, and the framework predicts that
		Responsibility
			Structural, not conventional: a chooser stands to its actualised states in a relation causal history cannot dissolve
		What this does not establish
			That human beings possess W (a further step, flagged as not yet made)
			The identification is only as good as its enumeration; exhibit a fourth mode of actualisation and it falls
	Reading the pair
		Take the requirement (proved) even if you decline the identification (argued)
		[? the second paper does not convince] you have taken what was established and declined what was proposed
	#TEG #freewill #Gödel #grounding
